const body = "---\ntitle: \"Stage 04: A task and a reference\"\ndescription: Write Recording your first track as a task and Supported audio formats as a reference, and learn why DITA has three information types.\ntype: tutorial\n---\n\n# Stage 04: A task and a reference {#stage-04-a-task-and-a-reference}\n\nAdd two information types to the guide. *Recording your first track* is a `<task>` with steps that lead to a result. *Supported audio formats* is a `<reference>` with a table of facts.\n\nConcepts explain a subject, tasks describe actions, and references provide information to look up. Each type has a content model that controls its structure. Validation checks that structure; you must also review whether the content serves the topic's purpose.\n\n**Time:** about 20 minutes. **You need:** stage 03 complete.\n\nRecorded diagnostic examples below come from earlier runs. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. Run the gate for your current checkout.\n\n## Step 1: Write the task {#step-1-write-the-task}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `topics/recording-your-first-track.dita`**\n\n   ```xml {title=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n   <task id=\"recording-your-first-track\">\n     <title>Recording your first track</title>\n     <shortdesc>Make your first recording in Audacity: pick a microphone, record, stop and play it back.</shortdesc>\n     <taskbody>\n       <context>\n         <p>This task walks you through making your first audio recording.\n         You need a microphone connected to your computer.</p>\n       </context>\n       <steps>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Open Audacity.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>On first launch Audacity opens an empty project window with a gray waveform area.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the Device Toolbar.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n             The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Record to start recording.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n             A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Stop when you are finished.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n         </step>\n       </steps>\n       <result>\n         <p>You now have a recorded audio track in your project.\n         The waveform shows the shape of the recorded audio: louder sounds appear as taller waves.</p>\n       </result>\n     </taskbody>\n   </task>\n   ```\n2. **Read the task elements**\n\n   - `<task>` and `<taskbody>` take the place of `<concept>` and `<conbody>`. The DOCTYPE changes with them: `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN`.\n   - `<context>` says why and when the reader does this task. It comes before the steps and holds ordinary paragraphs.\n   - `<steps>` is the procedure; each `<step>` is one action. The task body allows one `<steps>` (or `<steps-unordered>`, which stage 06 shows).\n   - `<cmd>` is the action itself, and is the one required child of a step. Write it as an imperative sentence. A reader who only reads the `<cmd>` elements should be able to complete the task.\n   - `<info>` is extra information about a step: what to expect, where to find the control. It is optional and sits after the `<cmd>`.\n   - `<result>` says what is true when the steps are done. It comes after the steps.\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Write the reference {#step-2-write-the-reference}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `topics/supported-audio-formats.dita`**\n\n   ```xml {title=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n   <reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n     <title>Supported audio formats</title>\n     <shortdesc>The audio formats Audacity imports and exports, and when to use each one.</shortdesc>\n     <refbody>\n       <section>\n         <p>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n         Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.</p>\n       </section>\n       <table>\n         <title>Common audio formats</title>\n         <tgroup cols=\"4\">\n           <colspec colname=\"format\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"type\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"quality\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"notes\" colwidth=\"2*\"/>\n           <thead>\n             <row>\n               <entry>Format</entry>\n               <entry>Type</entry>\n               <entry>Quality</entry>\n               <entry>Notes</entry>\n             </row>\n           </thead>\n           <tbody>\n             <row>\n               <entry>WAV</entry>\n               <entry>Uncompressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Large files, no quality loss.\n               Best for archiving and further editing.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>MP3</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n               Good for sharing and the web.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>OGG Vorbis</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Open alternative to MP3.\n               Good quality at low bitrates.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>FLAC</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Smaller than WAV with no quality loss.\n               Ideal for archiving when space matters.</entry>\n             </row>\n           </tbody>\n         </tgroup>\n       </table>\n     </refbody>\n   </reference>\n   ```\n2. **Read the reference elements**\n\n   - `<reference>` and `<refbody>`, with the DOCTYPE `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN`. A reference body is made of sections, tables and property lists rather than free paragraphs, so the introductory `<p>` sits inside a `<section>`.\n   - `<table>` uses the CALS table model. Use it for row or column spanning, a table title, or detailed column specifications. Its optional `<title>` supplies the table caption. Simple tables also support headers and relative column widths, as stage 05 explains.\n   - `<tgroup cols=\"4\">` declares the number of columns and holds everything else. One table can have several `tgroup`s with different column layouts.\n   - `<colspec>` names each column and sets its width. `colwidth=\"2*\"` is a proportional width: this column gets twice the share of the `1*` columns.\n   - `<thead>` and `<tbody>` hold header and body rows; each `<row>` holds one `<entry>` per column.\n:::\n\n## Step 3: Update the README and run the gate {#step-3-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Change the \"You are on\" line**\n\n   ```diff {title=\"README.md\"}\n   --- a/README.md\n   +++ b/README.md\n   @@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n    \n    Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n    \n   -You are on stage 03: first build.\n   +You are on stage 04: task and reference.\n    \n    ## Stages\n    \n   ```\n2. **Format and check**\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita\n   scripts/check-stage.sh\n   ```\n\n   ```\n   == validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n   3 file(s): 3 valid, 0 invalid.\n   …\n   STAGE OK\n   ```\n:::\n\nTo see the type split enforced, move the `<p>` in the reference out of its `<section>` so it is a direct child of `<refbody>`, and run the gate. The reference body does not allow it. A concept body would.\n\nAfter each error exercise, undo the deliberate change and rerun the gate. Confirm that it prints `STAGE OK` before continuing.\n\n## Publish your changes {#publish-your-changes}\n\nAdd the lesson's topics to `audacity-guide.ditamap`, then rebuild the guide. The complete map at this checkpoint is:\n\n```xml {title=\"audacity-guide.ditamap\"}\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Audio user guide</title>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n</map>\n```\n\n```bash\ndita --project=project.json --output=out\npython3 scripts/check-output-links.py out\n```\n\n## What you learned {#what-you-learned}\n\n- A task is `<task>`, `<taskbody>`, `<context>`, `<steps>`, `<step>`, `<cmd>`, `<info>`, `<result>`; the `<cmd>` is the step.\n- A reference is `<reference>`, `<refbody>`, sections and tables; no loose paragraphs.\n- The CALS table: `<table>`, `<tgroup cols>`, `<colspec>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<row>`, `<entry>`.\n- The three types provide content models for different reader needs.\n\n## Next lesson {#next-lesson}\n\nContinue with [Stage 05: inline and block](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/part-1-topics/stage-05-inline-and-block).\n\nFor the core course, use the [learning path](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path) to skip optional modules.\n\n[Compare this stage with its predecessor](https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial/compare/tutorial/03-first-build...tutorial/04-task-and-reference).\n";
